; LOADBYTECODE: the other half of examples/bytecode_save.logo -- run
; that file first (it writes scratch_bytecode.lgb), then run this one.
; No compile step happens here at all: LOADBYTECODE reads the file,
; assembles it, and runs it -- reproducing SQUARE, the same
; "drawing-a-square" output, and (since the saved program's own last
; statement was SAVEBYTECODE) writing the file to itself again too, all
; without this process ever having seen bytecode_save.logo's own
; source.

LOADBYTECODE "scratch_bytecode.lgb
DELETEFILE "scratch_bytecode.lgb

; A bytecode file doesn't have to come from SAVEBYTECODE at all -- it's
; just text, in the format docs/BYTECODE_REFERENCE.md documents.
; examples/hand_assembled.lgb was written by hand and never touched the
; Logo compiler; LOADBYTECODE runs it exactly the same way either way.
LOADBYTECODE "examples/hand_assembled.lgb
